# Whether a value t of T is a member of an interval with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One
# enclosure interface", the row of contains(t)): x_l <= t, t <= x_u, and t finite. Members are real
# numbers: +inf is not a member of [1, +inf] although both comparisons hold, hence the test of
# finiteness; a NaN fails every comparison, and the empty set (+inf, -inf) has no member. Exact. t
# is a value of the program, only compared (rule R3 asks no guard). For every precision.
kernel:        interval_contains_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
input:         t
output:        mask m
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       certain m member t x x=xl,xu exact
min_precision: 1
source:        the bounds compared with the point, and a test that the point is finite (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the result is a mask, which has no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; certain states what comes out for every canonical operand

a  = le(xl, t)
b  = le(t, xu)
ta = abs(t)
ft = lt(ta, const inf)
ab = and(a, b)
m  = and(ab, ft)
